1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,674
Bonjour. This is Liam, in for Bear. Anthropic worked with dozens of teams building

2
00:00:04,694 --> 00:00:08,697
agents, and the most successful ones used simple patterns, not complex frameworks.

3
00:00:08,717 --> 00:00:13,391
So don't ask for the smartest agent. Ask for the simplest one that works.

4
00:00:13,461 --> 00:00:17,671
Three terms. An augmented LLM: a language model with retrieval, tools, and memory

5
00:00:17,691 --> 00:00:22,227
attached. A workflow: models and tools run along paths written in code ahead of

6
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:27,108
time. And an agent: the model directs its own steps and its own tool use.

7
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:31,873
Everything starts with one station: a model with a kit. Retrieval to look things up,

8
00:00:31,893 --> 00:00:36,587
tools to act, and memory to keep what matters. Every pattern after this is built

9
00:00:36,607 --> 00:00:38,159
from stations like this one.

10
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:42,677
Pattern one: prompt chaining. Split the job into fixed steps. Each call works on the

11
00:00:42,697 --> 00:00:47,443
output of the one before, and a gate between them checks that it's still on track.

12
00:00:47,463 --> 00:00:48,634
Outline, check, then write.

13
00:00:48,704 --> 00:00:53,490
Pattern two: routing. Classify what comes in, then send it down the line built for

14
00:00:53,510 --> 00:00:58,297
it. Refunds go one way, tech support another. And easy questions can go to a

15
00:00:58,317 --> 00:00:59,258
smaller, cheaper model.

16
00:00:59,328 --> 00:01:03,994
Pattern three: parallelization. Split the belt, run the calls at the same time, and

17
00:01:04,014 --> 00:01:09,016
merge the results in code. Sectioning runs different parts of the job side by side.

18
00:01:09,036 --> 00:01:12,698
Voting runs the same task several times and compares the answers.

19
00:01:12,768 --> 00:01:17,098
Pattern four: orchestrator-workers. A central model reads the job, decides the

20
00:01:17,118 --> 00:01:22,634
subtasks on the spot, hands them to worker models, and pulls their results together.

21
00:01:22,654 --> 00:01:25,797
Unlike parallelization, the pieces aren't fixed in advance.

22
00:01:25,867 --> 00:01:29,950
Pattern five: evaluator-optimizer. One call makes the work. Another judges it

23
00:01:29,970 --> 00:01:35,173
against clear criteria and sends it back with notes, round and round, until it

24
00:01:35,193 --> 00:01:35,546
passes.

25
00:01:35,616 --> 00:01:40,366
Now take the rails away. That's an agent. It plans and picks its own route, one tool

26
00:01:40,386 --> 00:01:45,416
call at a time, checking real results, like a test run, at every step. And it gets a

27
00:01:45,436 --> 00:01:47,941
stop condition, such as a maximum number of steps.

28
00:01:48,011 --> 00:01:52,359
So which one should you build? Start with the simplest. Often one good call with

29
00:01:52,379 --> 00:01:56,727
retrieval and examples is enough. Every added step costs time and money, so add one

30
00:01:56,747 --> 00:01:59,056
only when you can measure that it helps.

31
00:01:59,126 --> 00:02:03,457
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here's a task I do every week: [describe it in

32
00:02:03,477 --> 00:02:06,448
two sentences]. Using the patterns in Anthropic's "Building effective agents", pick

33
00:02:06,468 --> 00:02:11,343
the simplest one that fits, from a single call up to a full agent. Tell me what a

34
00:02:11,363 --> 00:02:15,422
simpler pattern would get wrong, and what I should measure to prove the extra step

35
00:02:15,442 --> 00:02:19,501
helps. Then check two things yourself. Can you point to a real case where the

36
00:02:19,521 --> 00:02:23,308
simpler version fails? And did you run the simplest version on five real examples

37
00:02:23,328 --> 00:02:25,211
first? If it already works, stop there.

38
00:02:25,281 --> 00:02:29,923
Five Ways to Wire an Agent. At Nik Bear Brown.
